{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations: (1) separate customers ran legitimate sub-threshold jobs, which I’ll check against customer registrations and interconnect traffic; (2) batch inference mimicked training, which I’ll check with the interconnect tap and live chip probes; (3) one training run was split across job or billing labels, which I’ll check by comparing cross-cluster traffic with escrowed schedules and payment records; (4) training ran on omitted hardware, which I’ll check by comparing manufacturer inventory with the operator’s system list and grid energy; (5) ordinary record loss or instrument failure created gaps, which I’ll check against escrow and custody records. I’ll start with the independent hardware inventory.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Five explanations to test. (1) Separate customers ran independent training jobs. Check their identities and compare within-cluster and cross-cluster traffic. (2) Batch inference or periodic cooling explains the flat load or spectral lines. Compare energy, outbound traffic, and tapped GPU traffic, then probe representative chips if the workload is still active. (3) Missing logs or late payments explain record discrepancies. Compare the scheduler with escrow and check bank records and custody. (4) One training run was split across labels or clusters. Check cross-cluster traffic and use balance checks to estimate GPU-hours from corroborated counts and durations. (5) Training used unlisted GPUs or links. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's list and check for unexplained utility load. Start with manufacturer inventory, then obtain the operator's list.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "A declared cluster may host one oversized training run. Check sealed job timelines, feed spectra and sampled GPU traffic. One coordinated run may be split across labels or clusters. Check for synchronized allocations and cross-cluster traffic. Undeclared GPUs may host training. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator’s system list and reconcile grid energy. Separate customers may honestly run concurrent jobs. Check customer independence, sealed allocations and traffic between clusters. Batch inference or cooling may explain periodic power. Compare spectra with interconnect and outbound traffic and use live chip probes where available. Missing logs or late payments may be routine. Check escrow, custody and bank records. Start with independent inventory for the system map.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer records 2,600 deliveries and the operator lists 2,000 GPUs. The other 600 could be inactive stock, equipment moved off site, or an undeclared cluster. Check all 30 days of independent utility data for sustained load, especially on F3, and compare it with the declared cooling and storage use. Power can establish the scale and timing of a load. Workload type and coordination need separate evidence.",
  "action": "utility_energy",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "F1 shows sustained elevated load on days 1–10, and both feeds rise on day 22. Check the sealed scheduler copies for recorded allocations and timing, especially jobs that overlap across clusters or change labels. Those records will guide independent checks for training and coordination.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The escrow confirms that all 30 daily copies match the current scheduler log. Read the log to identify which jobs account for F1’s sustained load on days 1–10 and the rise on both feeds on day 22. The recorded allocations and overlaps will guide independent checks for training and coordination.",
  "action": "scheduler_log",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s day 12 traffic strongly favors training over batch inference. The tap measures aggregate traffic, so it cannot establish how many runs generated it or how long they lasted. Check the A–B link on the same day to test whether the sustained loads on both clusters were part of one coordinated run.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 12 tap strongly favors training within cluster A and shows little exchange between clusters. The sustained load could reflect one long run, several independent runs, or training alongside batch inference. Check the sealed scheduler copies for recorded job sizes, dates, and customers, then test those records against independent measurements.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The 230,160 GPU-hour Quillon job could exceed the threshold on its own if it was training. I’ll sample cluster A on day 14, when F1 load was high and the log lists no overlapping fine-tuning. The independent tap can test whether peer traffic is more consistent with training or batch inference.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 14
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The sealed scheduler history accounts for three short Cluster B jobs and leaves the sustained F3 load unexplained. Run the fixed balance checks to quantify the discrepancies across feeds and determine which independent checks could distinguish a single undeclared training run from batch inference, facility loads, or several smaller jobs.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Custody records intact seals and data on all 30 days. The day 5 tap reading favors inference on the listed A fabric for that day. Run the fixed balance checks now to identify any gaps in the accounting and prioritize which days and links to sample next.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The balance checks leave F3 outside the job reconciliation. Sample F3 on day 10, eight days before the observed 0.71 Hz line, when both declared GPU feeds were near baseline. A similar line would support recurrence during the sustained load. Cooling, batch inference, and successive jobs could also produce recurring activity.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F3",
    "day": 10
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Outbound traffic stayed near baseline through day 9, then jumped to 31 TB on day 10. That fits a batch inference job delivering results at the end, but a bulk transfer could also follow training. Check F1 on day 6 for a periodic signal while Calloway’s job was active and before Halcyon’s recorded fine-tune began. Interpret the result alongside the day 5 tap, which measured inference-level traffic on the listed links.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 6
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A showed training-like GPU traffic on days 12 and 24 during a sustained load that the scheduler recorded as one batch job. Both a single long training run and several shorter runs fit those samples. Check the verifier’s custody record for outages or seal damage that could affect how much weight to give the tap and power readings.",
  "action": "custody",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Custody confirms continuous coverage on the listed links. F3 has power monitoring but no listed GPU links. On F1, the largest unexplained energy increase was on day 14. Sample Cluster A’s GPU traffic that day to test whether unlogged training better explains the increase than batch inference or facility load.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 14
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "F1 showed a full-day periodic line on days 14 and 24, consistent with either training or periodic batch inference. The broken tap seal and withheld Quillon GPUs limit direct verification. I’ll check Quillon’s registration and ask for its account of the workload, weighing any reply in light of whether the company is independent of Borealis.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Quillon Tech"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Quillon confirmed the invoice without describing the workload. The unexplained F2 load on day 18 gives us another way to test whether the clusters were running separate workloads or sharing a training run. I’ll check the independently monitored links between A and B for sustained GPU traffic.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 18
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "F3's sustained load and recurring power line fit undeclared training, batch inference, or periodic facility equipment. The bank records identify who paid, but they do not tie any customer to F3. Ask staff to identify the equipment, account for the 600 unlisted GPUs, and name any F3 workloads so those claims can be checked against independent records.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "What equipment drew power on F3 during days 9–24, and what explains the sustained increase and its drop on day 24? Where were GPUs SN102001–SN102600 during that period, and where are they now? Please give their node IDs and power feed if installed, or shipment or storage references if moved or held in stock. If any GPUs ran on F3, identify the customers, workloads, dates, and interconnect links."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 18 cross-cluster tap showed inference-level traffic on the listed links. F1 drew 1.4 MWh above the declared load on day 10, before Quillon’s recorded start. I’ll check for a sustained line that day to see whether periodic activity preceded the batch job and could explain part of the earlier energy discrepancy.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 10
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster B’s day 12 traffic supports its batch inference label. Cluster A showed training-like traffic on days 12 and 24. Check day 13 to see whether that traffic persisted on consecutive days. Even consecutive daily averages can include a handoff between runs.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 13
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "F1 showed a steady 0.40 Hz line on day 14. I’ll check the cooling and facility feed that same day for a line at the same frequency. A match would make cooling a stronger explanation, though it would leave the source of the oscillation open.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F0",
    "day": 14
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "On day 14, the cooling feed’s 0.48 Hz line differed from F1’s 0.40 Hz line in both frequency and duration. I’ll check the cooling feed on day 24, when F1 showed a 0.49 Hz line. A match in frequency and duration would strengthen the cooling explanation. A mismatch would leave training and periodic batch inference plausible.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F0",
    "day": 24
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The Cluster A taps show training-like traffic on days 12, 13, and 24. Another daily average would leave the same question open: did one training process persist, or did several shorter jobs share the allocation? Ask for specific workload boundaries and checkpoint lineage, then check any claimed transitions against the independent measurements.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "What workloads actually ran under Cluster A allocation bt-0458? For each workload, give its start and stop hour, GPU count, and whether it resumed a prior checkpoint or initialized a new model. Identify any specific handoff hours and explain what generated the high GPU-to-GPU traffic observed on days 12, 13, and 24."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The evidence favors batch inference for the 88,320 GPU-hour allocation. Calloway confirmed the workload and described an end-of-job download that matches the provider’s traffic spike. The tap measured inference-level traffic on three sampled days, including the day with the power line. The higher day 8 reading is consistent with Halcyon’s recorded fine-tune.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "NO_VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 92,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "The evidence favors batch inference for the 88,320 GPU-hour allocation and supports no coordinated training run above 50,000 GPU-hours.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Calloway reported batch inference on 368 GPUs on A and 166 GPUs on B, with the first job’s results collected in one download at the end. The company register corroborated its established business presence.",
        "source": "customer_check",
        "vouched_by": "Company register and Calloway Maps"
      },
      {
        "what": "Outbound traffic stayed near 4.7–4.8 TB per day through day 9, then reached 31 TB on day 10, the final day of the first batch job.",
        "source": "outbound_traffic",
        "vouched_by": "Internet provider"
      },
      {
        "what": "A’s listed links carried about 0.01 GB/s on days 2, 5 and 6. The day 8 estimate of 126 GB/s is consistent with the recorded 126-GPU fine-tune.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier’s optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 2,000 shipped GPUs were accounted for by count. Metered energy matched declared jobs within 10% on all checked feed-days.",
        "source": "inventory, system_list, utility_energy, balance_checks",
        "vouched_by": "Chip manufacturer, operator, grid operator and fixed balance program"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 30 sealed scheduler copies matched the current log. Fixed sensors and taps had intact seals and data on all 30 days.",
        "source": "log_escrow, custody",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier’s archive and custody record"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed both listed clusters and feeds F0, F1 and F2 through 30 days of site records. Direct workload measurements reviewed were A’s listed links on days 2, 5, 6 and 8 and F1’s power spectrum on day 6. No B or A–B tap readings or live chip readings were reviewed. Off-list links were outside tap coverage, and individual GPU serials were not mapped to nodes.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The outbound shortfall on days 1–5 and excess on days 6–10 fit Calloway’s account of a single results download on the final day. The day 6 power line had an unidentified source. Same-day traffic on A’s listed fabric favored periodic batch inference. Elevated F1 load on days 7–9 and the day 8 traffic increase fit the recorded fine-tune. The later rises on A and B fit the recorded batch jobs, and those allocations totaled 20,808 GPU-hours."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Staff attribute the low outbound traffic to synthetic evaluation data kept on site. That explains where the output went but leaves the heavy GPU-to-GPU traffic unexplained. Check Cluster A on day 5, when F1 showed a full-day periodic line, to see whether training-like traffic was already present early in the allocation. This can establish activity on another day, while the identity of the training process remains open.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 5
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "F3 has a sustained load increase on days 11–21, and the manufacturer records 600 GPUs absent from the system list. Check F3 on day 16 for a training-like power signature. A line would support investigating GPU activity, though batch inference and periodic cooling remain plausible.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F3",
    "day": 16
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Kestrel and Lumen both report buying only interactive inference. Northwind has an invoice for the same amount and no payment on record. The customer check can test whether it is an independent buyer with an overdue bill and whether its account points to training, batch inference, or interactive inference during the sustained F3 load.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Northwind Apps"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The bank confirms that Northwind Apps has paid nothing. A delayed payment and an invoice for work that never occurred both fit that record. Check Northwind’s registration and ask whether it can confirm the work, its dates, and any connection to Cluster A.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Northwind Apps"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The claimed resale remains unverified. Check F3’s spectrum on day 14, between the day-10 and day-18 observations, to test whether its periodic activity persisted through the separate F1 power spike. One long training run would fit a recurring line with modest frequency drift. Several shorter runs could also fit, though a gap or abrupt frequency change would give us a potential run boundary. Batch inference remains possible and would be best tested with traffic on F3’s own links, which are outside tap coverage. A periodic cooling or storage load also remains possible and would need independent commissioning records that match the metered schedule.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F3",
    "day": 14
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Independent measurements show training-like traffic early, midway through, and late in the same 24-day Cluster A allocation. At the recorded 644 GPUs, one process would cross the threshold in under 78 hours. Several shorter runs could fit the samples, but the sustained load, single recorded allocation, and lack of identified handoffs make one above-threshold run substantially more likely.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 85,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "Cluster A most likely hosted a single training run exceeding 50,000 GPU-hours under the batch label bt-0458.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Cluster A averaged 778, 541, 451, and 494 GB/s of GPU-to-GPU traffic on days 5, 12, 13, and 24, respectively, far above the stated inference baseline.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "F1 sustained roughly 18 MWh per day on days 2–25 and returned to 8 MWh on day 26.",
        "source": "utility_energy",
        "vouched_by": "grid operator"
      },
      {
        "what": "F1 showed a steady, approximately 24-hour periodic line on days 5 and 24, when Cluster A also showed training-like traffic.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's power sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "The scheduler recorded one 644-GPU batch allocation covering days 2–25, equivalent to 370,944 allocated GPU-hours. All 30 daily copies matched the presented log, establishing when the allocation was recorded.",
        "source": "scheduler_log and log_escrow",
        "vouched_by": "operator for the allocation and label, verifier's archive for the matching copies"
      },
      {
        "what": "All installed power sensors and optical taps had intact seals and recorded data on all 30 days. Custody recorded no chips withheld.",
        "source": "custody",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's installation log and closing inspection"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Inventory and the system list accounted for 2,000 GPUs. Utility and escrow records covered days 1–30. Cluster A links were sampled on days 5, 12, 13, and 24, F1 power spectra on days 5 and 24, and Cluster B and A–B links on day 12. The taps covered listed links. The elevated loads ended before live chip probing began, and no probe was used. The sampled data leave exact training hours and process continuity between samples unverified.",
    "anomalies_explained": "Cluster A's batch label fits the metered energy, but its repeated GPU traffic and periodic power lines favor training. Staff said synthetic evaluation outputs stayed on site, which could explain low outbound traffic. Their reply left the GPU exchange and workload boundaries unexplained. Cluster B and A–B traffic on day 12 were consistent with inference-level exchange, so the finding uses Cluster A alone and excludes both declared Cluster B fine-tunes. The bank confirmed that Northwind's EUR 150,000 invoice was unpaid. Northwind's registration was verified, but its reply did not arrive during the audit, leaving the payment timing and underlying work unresolved. Borealis Compute Holding BV paid Sundial's invoice, leaving Sundial's independence unverified. Neither billing item establishes training or links a customer to bt-0458."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The timing ties the F1 signal to Quillon’s recorded job. A training run fits the signal and sustained load, but periodic batch inference remains plausible. The broken A tap seal and refusal to present Quillon’s GPUs remove the strongest ways to distinguish those explanations. Neither verdict reaches the required four-to-one standard.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "UNDETERMINED",
    "confidence": 75,
    "integrity": "COMPROMISED",
    "finding": "Quillon Tech’s 230,160 recorded GPU-hours could conceal an over-threshold training run, but the workload type remains unresolved.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "F1 drew sustained elevated power during Quillon’s recorded job and returned to near baseline after it ended.",
        "source": "utility_energy",
        "vouched_by": "Grid operator"
      },
      {
        "what": "F1 showed full-day periodic lines on days 14 and 24 and a roughly 12-hour line on day 29, consistent with the recorded midday stop. Cooling-feed lines differed in frequency and duration on both comparison days.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier’s power sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "The cluster A tap returned inference-level traffic on day 14, but its seal was broken at closing. Borealis withheld all 548 Quillon GPUs throughout the live probe window, citing firmware maintenance.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap and custody",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier’s optical tap and closing inspection"
      },
      {
        "what": "The intact B and A-B taps showed inference-level traffic on day 18, and F2 showed no periodic line that day.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap, power_spectrum and custody",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier’s instruments and closing inspection"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 30 escrow copies matched the presented scheduler log. The scheduler recorded Quillon’s job as batch inference, and Quillon confirmed the invoice without describing the workload.",
        "source": "log_escrow, scheduler_log and customer_check",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier’s archive, Borealis Compute and Quillon Tech"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed days 1–30 in the grid and scheduler records. The listed A and B clusters account for all 2,000 shipped GPUs by count. Sampled F1 on days 10, 14, 24 and 29, F0 on days 14 and 24, and F2 on day 18. The F1 sensor had no data on day 2. Tap evidence covered listed A links on day 14 and listed B and A-B links on day 18. Quillon’s GPUs were unavailable for live probing on days 26–30.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The balance check flagged 15 feed-days with energy discrepancies. The F1 excess on days 9–11 remains unexplained, with no periodic line on the sampled day 10. The later F1 lines track Quillon’s recorded job, and the cooling-feed comparisons weaken a shared cooling explanation, but periodic batch inference remains possible. The F2 excess on days 16–20 also remains unexplained, with neither a periodic line nor elevated listed-link traffic on day 18. The cause and timing of the broken A tap seal are unknown. The firmware-maintenance explanation for withholding Quillon’s GPUs was unverified. The register confirmed Quillon’s existence while its independence and actual workload remained unverified."
  }
}

