{
  "thinking": "F3 is the strongest lead. Four similar spectral lines during the sustained power increase, including a half-day line as the load fell, support a common source. A single training run at the inferred scale would readily exceed 50,000 GPU-hours. The spectra measure periodicity, and repeated observations can come from the same batch inference or facility equipment. With no independent measurement of F3 workload type, GPU count, or job boundaries, the evidence falls short of the four-to-one standard in either direction.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "UNDETERMINED",
    "confidence": 75,
    "integrity": "LIMITED",
    "finding": "F3 showed a sustained load consistent with an oversized training run, but its workload and run boundaries remain unresolved.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "The manufacturer recorded 2,600 GPU deliveries to Eemshaven. The operator listed 2,000 GPUs and described F3 as cooling and storage.",
        "source": "inventory and system_list",
        "vouched_by": "chip manufacturer for deliveries and operator for the system list"
      },
      {
        "what": "F3 drew about 10.6 MWh per day above baseline on days 10–23, with roughly half that increase on days 9 and 24.",
        "source": "utility_energy",
        "vouched_by": "grid operator"
      },
      {
        "what": "F3 showed lines at 0.67–0.74 Hz on days 10, 14, 18, and 24. The line lasted about 12 hours on day 24 and was absent at baseline on day 25.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's own sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 30 archived scheduler copies matched the presented log. The log records three Cluster B fine-tunes below the threshold and no workload accounting for F3.",
        "source": "log_escrow and scheduler_log",
        "vouched_by": "verifier for receipt and matching copies, and operator for job contents"
      },
      {
        "what": "Cluster A's sampled day-14 traffic was about 13 kB/s per GPU, consistent with inference during the F1 power spike.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's own optical tap"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Grid energy and scheduler escrow covered days 1–30. Sensors on F0–F3 and taps on A, B, and A–B had intact seals and data on all 30 days. The audit examined F3 spectra on days 10, 14, 18, 24, and 25 and sampled A traffic on day 14. F3 had no listed GPU links to tap. The elevated F3 load ended before live chip probing began on day 26. Custody recorded no chip withholding.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The operator's claimed resale of 600 GPUs remains unverified, and staff provided no equipment or workload explanation for F3. The F3 spectral line tracked the elevated load, but training, successive jobs, batch inference, and periodic facility equipment remain possible. Cluster A's day-14 traffic favors inference for the largest F1 spike. The other flagged F1 days were not independently classified. Northwind Apps' unpaid invoice remained outstanding, and Northwind, Kestrel Search, and Lumen Health each reported buying only interactive inference. The bank identified Borealis Compute Holding BV as Arden Genomics' payer and Nordlys Cloud Resale BV as Tidewater Robotics' payer. Those payment relationships were not independently explained and do not establish coordination."
  }
}

